    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "It does fix the problem for the UI but the internal state is outdated. If we have two sets of hits (regular, infinite) we can notice the difference. A possible solution when `showPrevious` is disabled might be to use the current page or fall back to 0. It would have the same behavior than `hits` + `pagination`.\r\n\r\nThe widget would have a weird behavior when another widget is removed though. The page won't be reset because no reset happens so it will stick at the current page (same as `hits` + `pagination`). The weird part is that the list gets reset (since a state change happens), which means that we are at page X without the previous results.",
    "commit_id": "f6cf349a792f941464e026a04d14ec72573fb551",
    "created_at": "2019-09-18T10:12:54Z",
    "diff_hunk": "@@ -242,20 +247,11 @@ const connectInfiniteHits: InfiniteHitsConnector = (\n           );\n         }\n \n-        if (!hasShowPrevious) {\n-          return widgetSearchParameters;\n-        }\n-\n-        if (uiState.page) {\n-          // The page in the search parameters is decremented by one\n-          // to get to the actual parameter value from the UI state.\n-          return widgetSearchParameters.setQueryParameter(\n-            'page',\n-            uiState.page - 1\n-          );\n-        }\n+        // The page in the search parameters is decremented by one\n+        // to get to the actual parameter value from the UI state.\n+        const page = hasShowPrevious && uiState.page ? uiState.page - 1 : 0;",
